      <p class="eyebrow"><span class="dot dot-monitor" aria-hidden="true"></span>Design Contract</p>
      <h1>Signal Transparency &amp; Master-Only Architecture</h1>
      <p>
        Perception is engineered around two non-negotiable invariants that keep what you see on
        screen identical to what reaches your monitors and export file.
      </p>

      <div class="prose-callout">
        <p>
          <strong>Total Signal Transparency:</strong> Every module, cable, gain, EQ, compressor,
          limiter, and send is a visible node or control in the UI. There is no hidden limiter,
          clipper, normaliser, or automatic gain anywhere — Master included.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Playing and rendering take Master only</h2>
      <p>
        Both live playback and offline rendering advance the entire project graph forward one block
        at a time: every track, internal outlet send, stereo group bus, Space return, and Master
        process together.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Playing</strong> streams Master blocks directly to the audio device and writes no files.</li>
        <li><strong>Rendering</strong> writes Master's stereo output untouched to <code class="mono">song.wav</code> and plays nothing.</li>
        <li><strong>No hidden stems or pre-rendered note caches:</strong> nothing outside the running graph ever produces audio.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>One schema-validated JSON project</h2>
      <p>
        Every note, modular patch, cable, bus group, outlet send, and canvas coordinate is stored
        in a single human-readable JSON project file validated against <code class="mono">daw.td</code>.
      </p>

      <div class="prose-table-wrap">
        <table class="prose-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Key</th>
              <th>Meaning</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><code>title</code></td>
              <td>Arrangement title displayed in the studio header.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>tempo</code></td>
              <td>Beats per minute (20–400 BPM).</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>sampleRate</code></td>
              <td>Frames per second (8,000–192,000 Hz; default 48,000).</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>buses</code></td>
              <td>Named stereo groups with gain, 3-band EQ, sidechain compressor, and cycle-checked <code>output</code>.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>tracks</code></td>
              <td>Per-track instrument or routed patch, gain (0–4×), pan (−1 to +1), dry bus, outlet sends, positions, and notes.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h3>Internal outlet send example</h3>
      <p>
        Tap any internal module outlet inside a track's patch straight into a stereo bus while
        leaving its dry cable untouched:
      </p>
      <pre class="prose-code"><code>{
  "sendId": 1,
  "source": 3,
  "outlet": 0,
  "bus": "pads",
  "level": 0.25,
  "enabled": true
}</code></pre>

      <h3>Parameter followers</h3>
      <p>
        Every setting inside a <code class="mono">PatchInstrument</code> node declares one of three
        deterministic followers: <code class="mono">Constant</code>, <code class="mono">Pitched</code>
        (multiplied by the note's frequency), or <code class="mono">Struck</code> (multiplied by the
        note's velocity). Unknown components or settings fail validation immediately.
      </p>
    </article>
